STUDENT-PROPOSED AI PROJECT – Code + Report Instructions (Zip File)
Your project submission must follow the instructions below. Submissions that do not meet these
requirements will not be graded.
1. Submission Requirements
    • Submit your work before the deadline. Late or incomplete submissions may receive zero
credit.
    • Your submission must include all required components listed below.
2. Required Files
At a minimum, your submission must include:
    • Source Code (C, C++, Java, or Python)
    • Makefile (required for C/C++ projects)
    • README File
o Must include clear instructions to compile and run your program
    • Report (PDF only)
Code should be well-organized, readable, and properly documented. Use appropriate code
structuring and refactoring practices.
3. Report Requirements
The report must be a well-structured PDF document (maximum 4–5 pages) and include:
    • Problem Description and Understanding
Clear explanation of the problem and its significance
    • Methodology and Algorithm Selection
Description of the approach, algorithm(s) used, and justification
    • Results and Evaluation
Presentation and analysis of results, including metrics and visualizations
    • Conclusion and Future Work
Summary of findings, limitations, and possible improvements
    • Use of AI Tools
Clearly describe how AI tools (e.g., ChatGPT) were used, including:
o Stages where AI was used
o Sample prompts
o Contribution to the project
    • Reflection
Personal reflection on challenges, learning, and overall experience
• References
Properly cited sources used in the report
Important:
    • Please organize your code files and report into a single folder and compress it as a .zip
file. The file name must include your team members’ names. Example :
JohnDoe_JaneSmith_Project2.zip
    • Failure to follow the required naming convention will result in a 10-point deduction.
Evaluation Criteria
Projects will be evaluated relatively based on the complexity and difficulty of the problem
addressed, as well as the quality and clarity of the report and the implementation. Well-
documented code, clear explanations, and meaningful results will be key factors in the evaluation.



